        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.78,
            0.092,
            0.907,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": "回 口可"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.298,
            0.568,
            0.323
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.1 A Quick Recap of Decimals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.332,
            0.847,
            0.399
          ],
          "content": "Recall that decimals are the natural extension of the Indian place value system to represent decimal fractions $( \\frac { 1 } { 1 0 } , \\frac { 1 } { 1 0 0 } , \\frac { 1 } { 1 0 0 0 } ,$ and so on) and their sums."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.4,
            0.644,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": "For example, **27.53** refers to a quantity that has:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.425,
            0.259,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "• 2 Tens"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.448,
            0.329,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "7 Units (Ones)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.472,
            0.278,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": "5 Tenths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.495,
            0.329,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "3 Hundredths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.52,
            0.848,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": "We have already learned how to multiply and divide fractions. In this chapter, we will learn how to perform these operations with decimals. You will see that the procedures for multiplying and dividing decimals are natural extensions of the procedures for multiplying and dividing counting numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.625,
            0.517,
            0.704
          ],
          "content": "Jonali and Pallabi play a game. Jonali says a fraction and Pallabi gives the equivalent decimal. Write Pallabi’s answer in the blank spaces."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.709,
            0.518,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "Jonali goes to the market to buy spices. She purchases 50 g of Cinnamon, 100 g of Cumin seeds, 25 g of Cardamom and 250 g of Pepper. Express each of the quantities in kilograms by writing them in terms of fractions as well as decimals."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.842,
            0.517,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": "The fractions Jonali gave Pallabi have denominators 10, 100, 1000, and so on."
        },
        {
